# gh-850 latch VOID — LATCH ECONOMY skips without the gh-842 zombie void

⚠️ **IMPORTANT**

Both sibling green-cover guards in this action void the cover when zombie check-runs hold the gate and fall through to dispatch:

- the `skipIfGreenCi` guard above: `green + zombies → "re-dispatching to override the ghosts"` (gh-842, live fa #1457 ~2h BLOCKED on ghost `in_progress` checks);
- the `skipIfValidatedHead` latch-skip guard: `green + zombies → dispatching instead of arm-only` (gh-842).

This new economy block re-checks only `vProbe.green` and `continue`s — with no `headCheckRunZombies` check. That makes it reachable in exactly the deadlocked shape this PR family exists to fix:

1. PR carries `ai_validated`, head **unchanged**, completed-green dispatched run on the head **plus** zombie `in_progress` check-runs from a cancelled-queued run holding mergeState BLOCKED (rollup still reads green — the gh-842 live shape, where `validationRollupGreen` was true while ghosts held the gate);
2. `validate-fresh-masked-green` now admits the latched PR (this PR removed `ai_validated` from its `notLabels`), so the action runs;
3. the `skipIfGreenCi` guard detects the zombies and falls through to dispatch — but 20 lines later the economy block sees `latch && vProbe.green` and skips;
4. no rule ever dispatches, the zombies are never overridden → BLOCKED forever, unapproved lane (the gh-821 zombie sweep only rescues `ai_validating` holders, not latched PRs; `validate-armed` needs `pr_approved`).

Net effect: for latched PRs the gh-842 zombie void in the `skipIfGreenCi` guard is unreachable in this rule — the guard says "void, dispatch" and this block overrides it with "covered, skip" in the same action.

**Suggested fix** — apply the same zombie check here:

```javascript
if (!rule.skipIfValidatedHead &&
    (ticket.labels || []).indexOf('ai_validated') !== -1 &&
    vProbe && vProbe.green) {
    var econZombies = vHead0 ? headCheckRunZombies(effectiveRepoInfo, vHead0) : null;
    if (econZombies && econZombies.zombies.length) {
        console.log('  🧟 ' + key + ' latch cover VOID — zombie check-run(s) hold the gate (gh-842) — re-dispatching');
        // fall through to the dispatch guards below
    } else {
        console.log('  ⏭️  ' + key + ' latch covers the current head ...');
        continue;
    }
}
```

# gh-850 latch VOID — voids a VALID latch on probe degradation

⚠️ **IMPORTANT**

`vProbe.green === false` does not distinguish "no completed-green dispatched run on this head" from "the probe failed". Both `hasSuccessfulDispatchedRun` and `PROBE_WORKER_SOURCE` **fail OPEN** — their catch branches warn and return `green: false` — and the probe shape `{active, verdict, green, newest}` exposes no degraded/error flag.

So a transient `gh api` failure (rate limit, network blip) on a head that IS covered by a completed-green dispatched run passes this condition, and the block **strips a valid `ai_validated` latch** and re-dispatches — violating AC2's economy exactly on the failure path, once per transient error (self-heals only via the re-latch on the next green, after a wasted CI run). This is the destructively-acting-on-uncertain-evidence pattern the sibling guards avoid: the gh-842 guard comment states the convention explicitly — "a probe degradation keeps the legacy skip (retry next tick)".

**Suggested fix** — give the probe a degradation signal and refuse to void on it:

```javascript
// in PROBE_WORKER_SOURCE catch branch + the four helper catch branches:
// out.degraded = true (or return {degraded:true} from the helpers)
...
if ((ticket.labels || []).indexOf('ai_validated') !== -1 &&
    vProbe && !vProbe.green) {
    if (vProbe.degraded) {
        console.log('  ⏭️  ' + key + ' cover probe degraded — latch kept, retry next tick');
        continue;
    }
    // ...the strip
}
```

If plumbing a `degraded` flag through all five probe paths is too invasive for this PR, an acceptable minimal mitigation is to only strip when the probe positively returned a run list (e.g. expose `newest`/list length even when green is false) — but the current shape cannot tell the two cases apart at all.

# gh-850 latch VOID — real label write under DRY

💡 **SUGGESTION**

`github_remove_label` here is not guarded by `if (!DRY)`, unlike every other mutating call in this same action: the `validation_failed` park-clear above (`if (!DRY)` ~line 1936), the park comment (`!alreadyParked && !DRY` ~line 2182), and `dispatchCiWorkflow`, which exists precisely because of a live bug — "a DRY SM tick really dispatched CI … DRY means NO side effects" (fa 2026-09-27). DRY ticks do execute `validate_pr` actions (they reach `dispatchCiWorkflow`), so a dry tick on a latched PR with a bare head would really strip `ai_validated`.

```javascript
if (!DRY) {
    try {
        github_remove_label({ ... });
    } catch (eLatchVoid) { ... }
}
```

Note the pre-existing latch-skip arm's `github_add_labels` a few lines above has the same gap, so this is consistency-with-surrounding-code rather than a new class of bug — worth fixing both while here.

# AC3 tick 1 duplicates the AC1 validate-fresh test almost verbatim

💡 **SUGGESTION**

Tick 1 of this replay rebuilds the exact same agent as `AC1: validate-fresh re-validates a latched PR on a CI-absent moved head (real query)` — same `realSource` payload, same `prStatus`, same `runsCli()` mock — and re-asserts the same two things (`dispatched(...)` and the `ai_validated` strip). The AC3-specific value is all in tick 2.

Extract a shared helper, e.g. `runTick1BareMovedHead()` returning the agent, and have both tests use it — otherwise a future change to the tick-1 setup will be applied to one copy and the two drift, silently weakening the replay's fidelity to the live timeline.

```javascript
function tick1BareMovedHead() {
    var sm = makeSmAgent(Object.assign(config('a', 'b'), { ... }));
    sm.action({ jobParams: { owner: 'a', repo: 'b', rules: [realRule('validate-fresh')] } });
    return sm;
}
```

